     xmlns:terms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/"
     xmlns:EUCAIM="https://cancerimage.eu/ontology/EUCAIM#"
     xmlns:ontologies="http://bioontology.org/projects/ontologies/">
    <owl:Ontology rdf:about="https://cancerimage.eu/ontology/EUCAIM#">
        <dc:contributor>APHP</dc:contributor>
        <dc:contributor>CHAIMELEON</dc:contributor>
        <dc:contributor>DKFZ</dc:contributor>
        <dc:contributor>EuCanImage</dc:contributor>
        <dc:contributor>FORTH</dc:contributor>
        <dc:contributor>GUMed</dc:contributor>
        <dc:contributor>INCISIVE</dc:contributor>
        <dc:contributor>ProCAncer-I</dc:contributor>
        <dc:creator>LIMICS</dc:creator>
        <dc:language>English</dc:language>
        <dc:publisher>EUCAIM</dc:publisher>
        <dc:publisher>LIMICS</dc:publisher>
        <dc:subject>A Hyper-Ontology for semantic interoperability among heterogeneous standards/models in oncology and medical imaging.

This effort is part of the EUCAM project 
(https://cancerimage.eu/)</dc:subject>
        <dc:title>EUCAIM&apos;s Hyper-Ontology</dc:title>
        <terms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">Laboratory of Medical Informatics and Knowledge Engineering in e-Health (LIMICS). (2025). EUCAIM&apos;s Hyper-Ontology_V2.0. Zenodo. https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.10777925.</terms:bibliographicCitation>
        <terms:date xml:lang="en">December, 2025</terms:date>
        <terms:license>EUCAIM Hyper-Ontology is licensed under CC BY 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/)</terms:license>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">EUCAIM&apos;s Hyper-Ontology is a well-founded layered and modular domain and domain-application ontology that defines the essentials of oncology and medical imaging.

The Hyper-Ontology is developed using a hybrid iterative semantically and formally well-founded approach composed of two main strategies: bottom-up (initially based on the domain-specific knowledge provided by AI4HI: ProCAncer-I, INCISIVE, CHAIMELEON, and EuCanImage) and top-down (based on the mCODE specifications). The ontology model has been extended and enriched based on new datasets from the EUCAIM onboarding pocess.

Various cancer types are covered in the Hyper-Ontology (n=22), including prostate, breast, colon, liver, rectum, lung, colorectal, brain, sarcomas, bladder, pancreas, thyroid, kidney, cervix, bone, ovary, and stomach. 
The Hyper-Ontology supports various EUCAIM components, including the ETL, EUCAIM CDM and HealthDCAT-AP, image segmentation/annotation, and federated querying/processing.

Terminology version:
SNOMED-CT 2025
LOINC 2025
Race 2025
ICD10 2021
ICDO3 2020
NAACCR 2018
Cancer Modifier 2022
OMOP Genomic 2024

&gt;&gt;&gt; Citations:

El Ghosh, M., Kalokyri, V., Sambres, M., Vaterkowski, M., Duclos, C., Tannier, X., Tsakou, G., Tsiknakis, M., Daniel, C. &amp; Dhombres, F. (2024). Towards Semantic Interoperability Among Heterogeneous Cancer Data Models Using a Layered Modular Hyper-Ontology. In Formal Ontology in Information Systems (pp. 179-192). IOS Press

El Ghosh, M., Daniel, C., Duclos, C., Kalokyri, V., Charlet, J., Sambres, M., Tsakou, G., Tsiknakis, M., and Dhombres, F. (2024). Grounding a Hyper-Ontology on mCODE Conceptual Model and Foundational Ontologies for Semantic Interoperability in Oncology. FOAM_FOIS 2024.

El Ghosh, M., Kalokyri, V., Sambres, M., Vaterkowski, M., Duclos, C., Tannier, X., Tsakou, G., Tsiknakis, M., Daniel, C. &amp; Dhombres, F. (2024). From Syntactic to Semantic Interoperability Using a Hyperontology in the Oncology Domain. In Digital Health and Informatics Innovations for Sustainable Health Care Systems (pp. 1385-1389). IOS Press.</rdfs:comment>
        <owl:priorVersion>1.4 (September 2025)</owl:priorVersion>
        <owl:versionInfo rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2.0</owl:versionInfo>
    </owl:Ontology>
    


